  "Question": "What is the maximum value of \\(f(x)=x^2-2x\\) on \\([0,3]\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "3",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "\\(f'(x)=2x-2\\), with a stationary point at \\(x=1\\). Since \\(f(0)=0\\), \\(f(1)=-1\\), and \\(f(3)=3\\), the maximum value is \\(3\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Completing the square\n\nCompleting the square makes the size of \\(x-1\\) control the function value:\n\\[\nf(x)=(x-1)^2-1.\n\\]\nFor \\(0\\le x\\le3\\), we have \\(-1\\le x-1\\le2\\), so \\((x-1)^2\\le4\\). Thus \\(f(x)\\le3\\). Equality occurs at \\(x=3\\), where \\(f(3)=3\\), so the maximum value is \\(3\\).",
